WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:03.236
[music playing]

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:06.606
NARRATOR: In Mossel Bay,
South Africa, Alison Towner

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:10.543
and Enrico Gennari investigate
if speed is what makes

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.480
a great white's jaws so deadly.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:18.418
Other investigators have seen
how a bull shark's bite works.

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.453
Now, getting a bite
impression might

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.957
help them solve the mystery
of how a great white combines

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:25.458
bite force with thrust.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.098
[splash]

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.535
[inaudible]

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:38.571
[music playing]

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.411
[water splashing]

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.412
[cheering]

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:46.379
Oh, wow!

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:54.888
This shark just came out of
the water, vertical open mouth

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:59.058
almost like saying, look at
me, how majestic animal I am.

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:00.660
This is definitely
the biggest shark

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.330
that we've had so far, guys.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.599
He gave a big bite,
and he's still playing

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.203
around for a good two minutes.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:15.041
So we should have got some
very nice bite imprint.

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:19.412
[water splashing]

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.214
NARRATOR: They've
got their results.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:22.115
Get it up, got it.

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.450
NARRATOR: And they're revealing.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.350
Wow.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.552
Wow, wow.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:26.786
It didn't just bite it.

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.888
It launched out the water
with it in his mouth.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.623
We couldn't have asked for
better teeth impressions.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:31.524
Look at this.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.593
This is gold.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.530
[music playing]

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.499
NARRATOR: The deepest
bite impressions are most

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:41.701
likely from the initial strike.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.904
[water splashing]

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.574
We've got huge amounts
of teeth marks there.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.776
These being the top
teeth of the shark's jaw,

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.978
and then on the reverse
side, here we can

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:54.647
see the lower jaw perfectly.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:57.884
This shark has all of
her teeth in place.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:02.088
NARRATOR: We can only move
our bottom jaws for eating.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:05.425
A great white can move
both top and bottom.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.097
But for a shark
capable of decapitating

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.601
a seal in one bite,
the damage to the decoy

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.103
is surprisingly minimal.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.838
Maybe, they don't
have as much high bite

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.407
force as other species,
but be that the speed alone

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.676
and the impact of
the force of the hit

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:26.846
is more powerful than
the actual bite force.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:29.382
So there's lots of
complexity to tease apart.

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.417
Coming up.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.520
NARRATOR: White sharks
combine bite force

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:38.024
and thrust to attack their prey.

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:43.796
Their teeth are long, sometimes
close to seven inches.

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.099
With that many
daggers, this shark

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:48.868
may not need as much bite
force as a bull shark.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.975
While the bull shark's
teeth act like a buzz saw,

00:02:56.042 --> 00:03:00.313
a white shark's function
like a guillotine.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.882
Great white sharks
have 24 teeth on the top

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:04.784
and 26 on the bottom.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:06.853
You can liken it to a
whole series of steak

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.556
knives in a fish's mouth.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.157
The top teeth are the cutters.

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:13.493
They are highly
serrated, and they

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.930
are basically the teeth that saw
into the flesh of their prey.

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:20.800
The bottom teeth
are very different.

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.236
They're much more narrow
but equally as serrated.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.672
So as the bottom teeth
pin and hold the prey,

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.740
the top teeth cut
through the flesh.

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.742
And those mechanics
together make the bite

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:31.711
so powerful and so effective.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:35.114
[music playing]

